WEBVTT

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:34.640
<v Ira Glass>Maybe you saw this story in the news earlier this summer. A man's getting mauled by a shark, maybe 100 yards out from a beach near San Diego. He yells for help, yells "shark." A woman swimming near him also yells. And some guys hear it and swim towards the shark attack to rescue him, which they do. He lives. So, you know, happy ending.
<v Ira Glass>也许你今年夏天早些时候在新闻里看到过这个故事。在圣迭戈附近的一个海滩外约100码处，一名男子正遭到鲨鱼袭击。他大声呼救，喊着“有鲨鱼”。在他附近游泳的一名女子也大声呼喊。一些人听到了，便游向鲨鱼袭击的地方去救他，他们成功了。他活了下来，所以，你知道，这有个圆满的结局。

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:46.380
<v Ira Glass>But here's the question. I couldn't stop thinking about this. Who does that? Who hears somebody yell "shark" and swims towards the shark? What kind of person is that?
<v Ira Glass>但问题来了。我无法停止思考这件事。谁会这么做？听到有人喊“有鲨鱼”还会游向鲨鱼？那是什么样的人？

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:53.540
<v Cameron Whiting>So I work in real estate development for a company that's based out of LA and Brentwood. We build apartments, hotels, resorts, offices.
<v Cameron Whiting>我在一家总部位于洛杉矶和布伦特伍德的房地产开发公司工作。我们建造公寓、酒店、度假村和办公室。

00:00:53.540 --> 00:01:06.450
<v Ira Glass>Meet Cameron Whiting, 31, single, newly moved to San Diego. And he was there the morning of the shark attack with a group, the North County Ocean Swimmers, that meets four times a week to swim a mile, mile and a half, or more.
<v Ira Glass>这就是卡梅隆·怀廷，31岁，单身，刚搬到圣迭戈。鲨鱼袭击那天早上，他就在那里，和“北县海洋游泳者”小组的一群人在一起，他们每周聚会四次，游一英里、一英里半或更远。

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:12.040
<v Cameron Whiting>I was out in the water about 100 yards offshore, just past the breaking waves with another swimmer, Kevin Barrett.
<v Cameron Whiting>当时我离岸边大约100码，刚过碎浪区，和另一名游泳者凯文·巴雷特在一起。

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.580
<v Ira Glass>And you guys, had you finished your swim for the day?
<v Ira Glass>那么你们，那天已经游完泳了吗？

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:25.460
<v Cameron Whiting>Yep. We were done with our swim. Most of the group had made their way in. Some were in the shower. Some were kind of playing in the waves. Kevin and I were body surfing beyond the break, and that's when we heard the screaming.
<v Cameron Whiting>是的，我们游完了。大多数人都已经上岸了，有些人去冲淋了，有些人还在浪里玩。凯文和我在碎浪区外进行身体冲浪，就在那时我们听到了尖叫声。

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.450
<v Ira Glass>How far was it from you?
<v Ira Glass>离你们有多远？

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:39.470
<v Cameron Whiting>He was probably 50 to 75 yards from us, so just barely within shouting reach. It was hard for us to hear the screams, but the tone of it was so extreme that we knew something really bad was happening.
<v Cameron Whiting>他离我们大概50到75码，正好在呼喊声能听见的范围内。我们很难听清尖叫的内容，但那种语调极其强烈，我们知道肯定发生了非常糟糕的事情。

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:49.000
<v Ira Glass>So this is the moment I wanted to ask you about. Somebody yells "shark," and then you swim towards the shark. Could you just talk about that moment?
<v Ira Glass>这就是我想问你的时刻。有人喊“有鲨鱼”，然后你就游向了鲨鱼。你能谈谈那一刻吗？

00:01:49.000 --> 00:02:07.670
<v Cameron Whiting>Yeah, that's one of the moments that, in the four weeks since this has happened, that I just keep replaying. And in the moment, everything happens really quickly, and you don't really have a lot of time to psychoanalyze what's going on and what you're feeling. You just kind of react on instinct. But there are a few options in the moment. And I think, in the second it happened, first of all, Kevin Barrett, who was next to me, he just took off.
<v Cameron Whiting>是的，那是自这件事发生后的四周以来，我一直在脑海中反复回放的时刻之一。那一刻发生得非常快，你没有太多时间去分析到底发生了什么，以及你有什么感觉。你只是凭本能做出反应。但在那一刻有几个选项。我认为，在事情发生的瞬间，首先，在我身边的凯文·巴雷特，他直接冲了过去。

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:33.560
<v Ira Glass>Took off towards the shark, didn't hesitate. But Cameron did hesitate, because Cameron is not only a real estate guy, Cameron is actually this incredible swimmer, the fastest swimmer in this group of ocean swimmers, and also a trained lifeguard who still works as a part-time lifeguard in Los Angeles, where they put you through this rigorous boot camp training, 200 hours over 10 weekends, Lifeguard Academy, including all kinds of ocean rescues.
<v Ira Glass>他冲向了鲨鱼，没有任何犹豫。但卡梅隆犹豫了，因为卡梅隆不仅是个房地产从业者，他还是个游泳高手，是这群海洋游泳者中游得最快的人，也是一名受过训练的救生员，至今仍在洛杉矶兼职做救生员。在那里，他们要经过极其严格的新兵训练，在10个周末里进行200小时的救生学院培训，包括各种海上救援。

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:36.230
<v Cameron Whiting>They taught us in academy that slow is pro.
<v Cameron Whiting>学院里教导我们说，慢即是专。

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:40.110
<v Ira Glass>Slow is pro. Professionals stop and assess.
<v Ira Glass>慢即是专。专业人士会停下来评估。

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:48.220
<v Cameron Whiting>So yeah, there was that split second. But my head was just racing with all the scenarios, and it was, one, we need to make sure somebody is going to get backup.
<v Cameron Whiting>所以，确实有那么一刹那的停顿。但我的脑海里飞速闪过各种情景，第一点是，我们需要确保有人去寻求支援。

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:59.180
<v Ira Glass>Backup. He looks to the beach to see if anybody's running to call a medic. Looks like yes. On to scenario two, which he was thinking about because he was actually in the middle of getting recertified as a lifeguard.
<v Ira Glass>支援。他看向海滩，看是否有人在跑去叫医护人员。看起来是的。接着是第二个场景，他之所以会想到这个，是因为他当时正好在进行救生员的重新认证。

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:11.110
<v Cameron Whiting>So it's funny. You have to watch all these videos, and the video I had just watched the night before was how, as a first responder, you can't swim into-- or you shouldn't respond to an unsafe scene. Scene safety is number one.
<v Cameron Whiting>所以很有趣。你必须观看所有这些视频，而我前一天晚上刚看的视频讲的是，作为第一响应者，你不能游进——或者说你不应该响应一个不安全的现场。现场安全是第一位的。

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:28.400
<v Cameron Whiting>And in fact, through our lifeguard training, they even taught us in the academy manual that if there's a shark attack, you're not supposed to swim into it. You're supposed to call your supervisor and wait for help. But that just didn't make sense to me in the moment. I think the more important thing they taught us is that you have to make a judgment call in those difficult circumstances.
<v Cameron Whiting>事实上，在我们的救生员培训中，学院手册甚至教导我们，如果发生鲨鱼袭击，你不应该游进去。你应该通知主管并等待救援。但在那一刻，这对我来说行不通。我认为他们教给我的更重要的一点是，你必须在那些困难的情况下做出判断。

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:39.400
<v Ira Glass>What's so weird about that is that they're saying, like, use your judgment about whether to go in, but what does that even mean in this situation? Like, what data could you be using to make a judgment?
<v Ira Glass>最奇怪的是，他们说，要利用你的判断力决定是否进入，但在这种情况下，那到底意味着什么呢？比如，你到底能利用什么数据来做出判断？

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:50.560
<v Cameron Whiting>Yeah, for me, it was just years of experience swimming out there, and I felt comfortable. I've swum thousands of miles out there in way sharkier waters, and I've never seen or had an issue with a shark.
<v Cameron Whiting>是的，对我来说，那只是多年在海上游泳的经验，我感到很自在。我在那片海域游过几千英里，那里的鲨鱼更多，我从未见过鲨鱼或遇到过问题。

00:03:50.560 --> 00:04:10.550
<v Cameron Whiting>And so, even though there was a shark attack, I've heard that when there are shark attacks, usually they bite you, and they kind of spit you out after they realize you aren't what they wanted to eat. And so in my head, I think-- and again, I didn't have a ton of time to analyze this in the moment. But I think the risk of a shark coming back and trying to attack all of us was minimal.
<v Cameron Whiting>所以，尽管发生了鲨鱼袭击，但我听说过，当鲨鱼袭击时，它们通常咬一口，意识到你不是它们想吃的食物后，就会把你吐出来。所以在我的脑海里，我想——再次声明，我当时没有太多时间去分析这一点——但我觉得鲨鱼回来并试图攻击我们所有人的风险是很小的。

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:25.030
<v Ira Glass>Wow, that's so interesting. I think you see that differently than most of us civilians do. I mean, I think the thing that I've heard, and I feel like so many people have heard, is if there's blood in the water, the sharks are going to stick around because there's blood in the water, you know?
<v Ira Glass>哇，那太有趣了。我想你和我们大多数平民的看法不同。我的意思是，我想我听到过的，也是很多人都听到过的，就是如果有血水，鲨鱼会徘徊不走，因为水里有血，你知道吗？

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:38.570
<v Cameron Whiting>Totally, yep. I think it was, the shark made a mistake and realized it and probably swam away. And I think the conditions make a big impact, too. I think the morning of the incident, there was red tide, which is a red plankton that makes the water really murky and muddy. It was kind of choppy.
<v Cameron Whiting>完全同意。我认为是这样，鲨鱼犯了个错误，意识到后可能就游走了。而且我认为条件也有很大影响。我想事发那天早上，有赤潮，这是一种红色的浮游生物，会让水变得非常浑浊和泥泞。当时海浪有点大。

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:49.550
<v Cameron Whiting>And so in my mind, the shark attack happened because the shark probably couldn't see, and it couldn't tell what it was biting. And it attacked thinking it was a seal, because there was a seal out there that morning.
<v Cameron Whiting>所以在我看来，鲨鱼袭击是因为它可能看不见，无法辨别它在咬什么。它以为咬的是海豹，因为那天早上确实有一只海豹在那里。

00:04:49.550 --> 00:05:09.590
<v Ira Glass>All that said, he was still scared. He says sharks are always in the back of his head as an ocean swimmer. And for whatever random reason, for a couple of months before this day, he'd been having nightmares about sharks coming at him, emerging from the deep. Now he might be heading towards what he calls a 1,500-pound pitbull, agitated and fighting.
<v Ira Glass>尽管如此，他当时还是害怕的。他说作为一名海洋游泳者，他脑子里总是在担心鲨鱼。不知出于什么偶然原因，在那天之前的几个月里，他一直做噩梦，梦见鲨鱼向他袭来，从深渊中浮现。现在他可能正朝着他所说的“1500磅重的斗牛犬”游去，那头鲨鱼正焦躁不安并进行攻击。

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:20.390
<v Ira Glass>But in that quick moment or two before he took action, he took note of a couple of other things. He and Kevin were the closest to the guy who got bit, who was another swimmer from the group. No lifeguards were near.
<v Ira Glass>但在他采取行动前的那一两秒钟里，他注意到了其他几件事。他和凯文离那个被咬的人最近，那人也是小组里的另一名游泳者。附近没有救生员。

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:34.550
<v Cameron Whiting>And I think I just did the math in my head in that split second of, I think the thought of living for the rest of my life, knowing that I was in a position to help and not doing it, would have just eaten away at me way more than the thought of swimming into something that's dangerous.
<v Cameron Whiting>我想我当时那一瞬间在心里做了计算，我想，如果余生都知道自己有能力帮忙却没去帮，这会比游向危险更让我备受煎熬。

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:49.440
<v Ira Glass>That's funny when you weigh it up like this. I feel like the math of it actually seems really, really clear. You're like, I'm a really good swimmer. I'm trained for this. Other people are further away. And I really don't think the shark's going to bite me. Like, the math is so straightforward.
<v Ira Glass>当你这样权衡时，真是很有意思。我觉得这背后的逻辑其实非常非常清晰。你心想，我是个游泳好手，我受过这种训练。其他人都在更远的地方。而且我真的不认为鲨鱼会咬我。这逻辑太直白了。

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:57.990
<v Cameron Whiting>Yeah, in my mind, it didn't seem like a huge thing. I was just out there in the right place, in the right time. But again, Kevin's the one that really made that first move and inspired the courage in me to go.
<v Cameron Whiting>是的，在我心里，这看起来并不是什么了不起的事。我只是正好在那个时间出现在了那个地点。但话说回来，凯文才是那个真正采取行动并激发我勇气的人。

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:01.140
<v Ira Glass>And had Kevin done this kind of training, too?
<v Ira Glass>凯文也接受过这种训练吗？

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:09.750
<v Cameron Whiting>He hadn't. And he jokes that the reason he instantly responded the way he did out of instinct was because he didn't have the training. [LAUGHS]
<v Cameron Whiting>他没有。他开玩笑说，他之所以能凭借本能立即做出反应，就是因为他没有接受过那种训练。（笑）

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:13.950
<v Kevin Barrett>I didn't think. I really didn't. I just reacted.
<v Kevin Barrett>我没多想。真的没有。我只是做出了反应。

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:27.960
<v Ira Glass>This, of course, is Kevin Barrett, 51 years old the day he hurled his body through the water towards a shark attack-- married, two teenage sons. Pays the bills as a financial advisor, but also has a little startup trying to convert ocean waves and their motion into electricity.
<v Ira Glass>这当然就是凯文·巴雷特，那天他奋不顾身地冲向鲨鱼袭击现场时51岁，已婚，有两个十几岁的儿子。他做财务顾问赚钱，但也有一个小小的初创公司，试图将海浪及其运动转化为电能。

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:42.180
<v Ira Glass>He used to play rugby and do triathlons, but at some point switched to ocean swimming. And talking with him about why a person might swim towards a shark attack is pretty much the opposite day, upside down world, mirror universe, black-is-white version of talking with Cameron about it.
<v Ira Glass>他过去常打橄榄球和参加铁人三项，但在某个时刻转向了海洋游泳。和他谈论为什么一个人会游向鲨鱼袭击现场，这与和卡梅隆谈论这件事几乎是完全相反的世界，颠倒的世界，镜像宇宙，黑白颠倒。

00:06:42.180 --> 00:07:01.860
<v Kevin Barrett>It's almost, ignorance is bliss, right? I didn't have that knowledge set. And so for me, it was, my friend's in trouble. I just reacted and went to him. Right? I didn't have the same knowledge that Cameron had. So I just reacted.
<v Kevin Barrett>几乎是无知者无畏，对吧？我没有那套知识体系。所以对我来说，就是我的朋友有危险了。我只是做出了反应，就向他游去了。对吧？我没有卡梅隆那样的知识。所以我只是做出了反应。

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:18.270
<v Ira Glass>Well, it's funny. When he describes his thing, it's almost not heroic. He just did the math. You know what I mean? Like, whereas when you describe yours, it's way more heroic because you're just sort of throwing yourself at danger. Did you assume that the shark would probably swim away once it bit him that one time?
<v Ira Glass>嗯，真有趣。当他描述他的行为时，简直称不上英勇。他只是做了那个决定，你知道我的意思吗？而当你描述你的行为时，因为你只是把自己投向危险，这显得更加英勇。你当时是否认为鲨鱼咬了一口之后可能会游走？

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:25.800
<v Kevin Barrett>Honestly, I didn't. I really didn't think too much about the shark. I was really just focused on Caleb.
<v Kevin Barrett>老实说，并没有。我真的没太考虑鲨鱼。我当时满脑子想的只是凯勒布。

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:27.540
<v Ira Glass>Caleb, the guy who got bit.
<v Ira Glass>凯勒布，那个被咬的人。

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:35.410
<v Kevin Barrett>I was like, I need to get my friend, you know? He's injured. And how bad are his injuries? And let's get him to shore.
<v Kevin Barrett>我当时心想，我得救我的朋友，你知道吗？他受伤了。他的伤势有多严重？让我们带他上岸。

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:39.230
<v Ira Glass>Hmm. How well do you know Caleb?
<v Ira Glass>嗯。你和凯勒布认识多久了？

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:41.540
<v Kevin Barrett>I'd only met-- this was the third time I'd met him.
<v Kevin Barrett>我只见过他——这是我第三次见到他。

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:52.430
<v Ira Glass>The third time he'd met him. They'd chatted a little about rugby on the beach. Caleb also used to play. That is the extent of the friendship for which he risked his life. People, man.
<v Ira Glass>这是他第三次见到他。他们在海滩上聊过一会儿关于橄榄球的话题。凯勒布过去也打过橄榄球。这就是他为此冒着生命危险的友情的全部。人们啊，真是的。

00:07:52.430 --> 00:08:02.990
<v Ira Glass>Kevin and Cameron would both be very annoyed with me if I did not point out right now that other people were also critical to saving Caleb's life that day. There was the woman who stayed by him during the attack and yelled for help.
<v Ira Glass>如果我现在不指出那天还有其他人对拯救凯勒布的生命至关重要，凯文和卡梅隆都会对我感到非常恼火。当时还有一位女士在袭击期间一直待在他身边并大声呼救。

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:19.910
<v Ira Glass>Then, when they got to Caleb, he was bleeding badly, like a lawn mower hit his torso, Cameron said. The shark was gone, and a surfer approached with a surfboard. They put Caleb on, get him to shore. Then there were medics, of course, and others who helped. Caleb is fine now.
<v Ira Glass>当他们到达凯勒布身边时，他流血严重，卡梅隆说就像割草机撞到了他的躯干。鲨鱼已经走了，一名冲浪者带着冲浪板游了过来。他们把凯勒布抬上去，带他上岸。当然，还有医护人员和其他提供帮助的人。凯勒布现在很好。

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:36.660
<v Ira Glass>Cameron and Kevin both said that since this story hit the news, lots of people have come up to them, saying they could never have done what the two of them did. They would not have swum toward a shark. Cameron says he tells them, you'd be surprised at what you can do. Kevin tells them, of course you would.
<v Ira Glass>卡梅隆和凯文都说，自从这个故事登上新闻后，很多人走到他们面前说，他们绝对做不到他们俩做过的事。他们绝不会游向鲨鱼。卡梅隆说他告诉他们，你会惊讶于自己能做到什么。凯文告诉他们，你当然会。

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:50.640
<v Kevin Barrett>People, I think, are innately good. And we will do the right thing when pressed, right? And I'm just like you. And hey, we do good.
<v Kevin Barrett>我认为人本性是善良的。当我们受到压力时，我们会做出正确的事情，对吧？我只是和你一样的人。嘿，我们要做好事。

00:08:50.640 --> 00:09:01.740
<v Ira Glass>I don't know. I think you and I see people differently. I think that we are a mix of good and bad. And all the people who say, like, I wouldn't do that, I think that those people, many of them are telling the truth.
<v Ira Glass>我不知道。我觉得你看待人的方式和我不同。我认为我们是好与坏的混合体。对于那些说“我不会那么做”的人，我觉得其中很多人说的是实话。

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:18.300
<v Kevin Barrett>And I guess that's where we differ. I'm not any different to anybody else. And this is the reaction that I had. Trust that you would have the similar reaction. That's a cool thing to think about.
<v Kevin Barrett>我想这就是我们的分歧所在。我并不比任何人特别。这就是我做出的反应。要相信你也会有类似的反应。这想法挺酷的。

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:28.900
<v Ira Glass>That is a cool thing to think about. And we don't agree on this. I do think that there are some people who are like you and some people who aren't. But because this is your interview and not mine, you will get the last word.
<v Ira Glass>那确实是个很酷的想法。我们在这一点上没有达成共识。我确实认为有些人像你，有些人则不然。但因为这是你的访谈而不是我的，所以你会拥有最后发言权。

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:38.200
<v Kevin Barrett>[LAUGHS] OK. Good.
<v Kevin Barrett>（笑）好的。太好了。

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:50.170
<v Ira Glass>Today on our show, people who decide to put themselves in danger and swim towards sharks. Sometimes, they're doing it out of idealism, for the greater good of everybody, because they're good people-- if such a thing exists.
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，讲述的是那些决定让自己身陷险境并游向鲨鱼的人。有时，他们这样做是出于理想主义，为了所有人的更大福祉，因为他们是好人——如果世上真有这种人存在的话。

00:09:50.170 --> 00:10:17.580
<v Ira Glass>Sometimes, it's purely personal and the only way they know to fix some problem in their own life. Sometimes, the people around them do not understand why they're doing it at all. True stories of human peril and the people who seek it out. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>有时，这是纯粹个人的，是他们修复自己生活中某种问题的唯一途径。有时，周围的人根本不理解他们为什么要这样做。这是关于人类困境及其寻求者们的真实故事。来自芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:38.180
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. Act One, "If I Only Had My Brain." When Sarah was 15, she landed the lead role with a professional theater company playing Alice, the lead role in Alice Through the Looking Glass. And during the course of the production, she became petrified of going on stage, stage fright at an almost chemical level.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。第一幕，“如果我有大脑”。莎拉15岁时，在一家专业剧团担任主演，饰演《爱丽丝镜中奇遇记》中的爱丽丝。在制作过程中，她变得对登台表演感到极度恐惧，这种舞台恐惧症达到了近乎生理反应的程度。

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:49.860
<v Ira Glass>Every day, she says, the very thought of doing the show again drenched her in sweat. Her heart pounded so hard, she thought it might explode. She was afraid. And she was afraid people would discover she was afraid, which made it worse.
<v Ira Glass>她说，每天一想到要再次演出，她就汗如雨下。她的心脏剧烈跳动，她以为心脏可能会爆炸。她害怕。她还害怕别人会发现她很害怕，这让情况变得更糟。

00:10:49.860 --> 00:11:07.020
<v Ira Glass>And long story, but she lied her way out of the gig so she didn't have to do it anymore, which ended the play's run early, leaving her adult cast members who needed work out of work. She felt really bad about that, didn't own up to it for years after.
<v Ira Glass>故事很长，但她撒谎退出了演出，这样她就不必再表演了。这导致该剧提前结束，让需要工作的成年演员失业了。她对此感到非常难过，多年后才敢承认。

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:30.130
<v Ira Glass>The funny thing is, Sarah, she'd acted a bunch before this-- and after it, in movies, on TV, where there's no audience with you in the room. You may have heard of her, Sarah Polley. These days, she works behind the camera as a director and a screenwriter. But being on stage, after that one production, it was a shark she did not swim toward, one of several.
<v Ira Glass>有趣的是，在这之前和之后，莎拉在电影和电视中演过很多戏，那些地方没有观众和你在一起。你可能听说过她，莎拉·波莉。如今，她在幕后担任导演和编剧。但在那次制作之后，登台表演成了她没有游向的一条鲨鱼，她是几条鲨鱼中的一条。

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:49.360
<v Sarah Polley>Yes, I've never been somebody to push myself. In general, if something makes me anxious, I don't do it. And certainly, throughout my acting career, there are a lot of things I just backed right away from the second they started to make me anxious at all. Like, I just didn't want the trouble. It's time to move in a different direction.
<v Sarah Polley>是的，我从来不是一个会逼迫自己的人。通常情况下，如果某件事让我焦虑，我就不做。当然，在我的演艺生涯中，很多事情我一感到焦虑就马上退缩了。比如，我只是不想惹麻烦。是时候往另一个方向发展了。

00:11:49.360 --> 00:12:04.050
<v Ira Glass>And then, a couple of decades later, well into adulthood, something happened that just changed all of that, made her way less avoidant, fearful. And it wasn't a thing that you would expect. She told the story to one of our producers, Sean Cole.
<v Ira Glass>然后，几十年后，步入成年，发生了一件事彻底改变了这一切，让她变得不再那么逃避、恐惧。这并不是你预料之中的那种事。她把这个故事告诉了我们的制片人之一，肖恩·科尔。

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:24.340
<v Sean Cole>So it's October 2015. Sarah had just finished swimming, actually, at the local rec center and was getting ready to walk home, but her hair's wet and it's chilly outside. It's Toronto, where she lives. It gets cold. And she can't find her blow-dryer. Goes to the front desk, says, has anybody seen it? The woman says, meh, go check the lost and found bin.
<v Sean Cole>那是2015年10月。莎拉刚刚在当地的娱乐中心游完泳，正准备走回家，但她的头发是湿的，外面很冷。这是在多伦多，她居住的地方，天气很冷。她找不到吹风机了。她走到前台问，有人看见它吗？那个女人说，嗯，去看看失物招领箱吧。

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:35.740
<v Sean Cole>So she goes over to the corner of the lobby and crouches down to rummage around in the bin, above which, hanging on the wall, is this big industrial fire extinguisher.
<v Sean Cole>于是她走到大厅的角落，蹲下来在箱子里翻找，箱子上方墙上挂着一个巨大的工业灭火器。

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:59.230
<v Sarah Polley>I was overheating, and I stood up quickly to toss my winter coat off in a kind of tantrum. And I think what must have happened is my shoulder must have dislodged the fire extinguisher, and it crashed down on the left side of my head. So my memory after that is foggy.
<v Sarah Polley>我当时热得不行，快速站起来，想要发泄似地甩掉冬大衣。我想一定是我的肩膀碰到并移位了灭火器，它砸到了我的左侧头部。之后的记忆就很模糊了。

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:30.890
<v Sarah Polley>But what I do remember is, I felt like all my teeth were going to fall out of my head. I had a lot of thoughts about dentistry and how I wished I knew more about dentistry, because that seemed like a priority at that moment. And I kind of put together very slowly this terrible pain in my teeth and on the left side of my jaw and head with the fact there was this huge clang around the same time, and I could see the fire extinguisher rolling. And I realized I should probably let someone know.
<v Sarah Polley>但我记得的是，我觉得所有的牙齿都要掉出来了。我想了很多关于牙科的事情，真希望自己能多懂点牙科知识，因为那在当时看来是重中之重。我慢慢地把牙齿和左颚及头部的剧痛，与灭火器掉落时发出的巨响联系在一起。我看到灭火器在滚动。我意识到我可能应该让别人知道。

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:49.340
<v Sarah Polley>And so I started saying, in a very monotone voice, to no one in particular, a fire extinguisher just fell on my head. A fire extinguisher just fell on my head. And it was literally like listening to a robot. There was no affect in the voice that I heard coming out of me.
<v Sarah Polley>于是我开始用一种非常单调的声音，对着空气说，一个灭火器刚砸到了我的头上。一个灭火器刚砸到了我的头上。那听起来简直就像在听一个机器人说话。我听到的自己的声音里没有任何情感。

00:13:49.340 --> 00:14:01.500
<v Sean Cole>The woman behind the counter ran to get help, but instead of waiting around, Sarah just set off on her walk home, which wasn't easy. Somehow, her body kept veering to the right, onto people's lawns.
<v Sean Cole>柜台后的女人跑去寻求帮助，但莎拉没有等，而是动身回家，这并不容易。不知何故，她的身体一直向右倾斜，走到别人的草坪上去了。

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:20.770
<v Sarah Polley>I felt like I was underwater. I felt like everything was way too loud. But I also felt like, I have two tiny kids. I'm in the middle of a job. Nothing can have happened to me right now because that's just not possible. I just can't make room for that at the moment, so.
<v Sarah Polley>我觉得自己好像在水下。我觉得周围一切都太吵了。但我也觉得，我有两个小孩子。我正在工作中。不可能发生什么事情在我身上，因为那是不可能的。我现在实在无法接受那种可能，所以。

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:39.710
<v Sean Cole>You might have guessed by now that she'd suffered a concussion, in which case you know more about Sarah's brain than she did in those first few hours after the accident. For her, it was one of those moments where you're not sure yet how serious or benign an injury might turn out to be. And if you just act casual, maybe it'll get better on its own.
<v Sean Cole>你现在可能已经猜到了她受了脑震荡，如果是这样，那你比她事故后最初的几个小时里对自己大脑的了解要多得多。对她来说，那是那种你还不确定伤势会变得多么严重或良性的时刻。如果你只是装作若无其事，也许它会自行好转。

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:50.090
<v Sean Cole>But in the kitchen that night, making dinner, both her kids bouncing around, she started to experience her brain like a new appliance she wasn't used to yet and was kind of in awe of.
<v Sean Cole>但那天晚上在厨房做饭时，两个孩子蹦蹦跳跳，她开始体验到自己的大脑就像一个她还不习惯使用的新设备，让她感到敬畏。

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:58.400
<v Sarah Polley>Everything for me became scrambled. So I remember having the thought, how am I supposed to open the oven and turn it on if they're making so much noise?
<v Sarah Polley>对我来说，一切都变得混乱了。我记得当时想，如果他们发出这么大的噪音，我该怎么打开烤箱并启动它呢？

00:14:58.400 --> 00:14:59.390
<v Sean Cole>Wow.
<v Sean Cole>哇。

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:18.480
<v Sarah Polley>And I remember going, well, that's an odd thought, but I couldn't quite piece together why it was an odd thought. And I think the moment I really had to admit something was wrong was-- even though something was obviously wrong-- was I snapped at my three-year-old in a way that I just had never snapped before.
<v Sarah Polley>我记得自己心想，那是个奇怪的想法，但我无法完全理清为什么那是个奇怪的想法。我认为我真正不得不承认自己出了问题的那一刻是——尽管很明显出了问题——我对我三岁的孩子发了火，那种发火方式我以前从未有过。

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:34.680
<v Sarah Polley>And I remember holding these little shoulders and these eyes looking up at me, shocked. And then I became shocked because I was like, what am I doing? I mean, I wasn't rough, but it was way beyond how I had ever been.
<v Sarah Polley>我记得抓着那小小的肩膀，看着那双注视我的眼睛，感到震惊。然后我变得震惊，因为我想，我在做什么？我的意思是，我并不粗鲁，但这远远超过了我以前的程度。

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:54.800
<v Sarah Polley>And I remember just backing up and removing myself and going and sitting in a dark room and going, something's happened. I feel like I can cut through the air with my hand like it's butter, like it's a substance. And I remember saying to my husband, David, did reality always feel like this?
<v Sarah Polley>我记得只是退后，离开那里，坐进一个黑暗的房间，心想，发生了什么事。我觉得我可以像切黄油一样切开空气，好像它是一种实质性的东西。我记得我对丈夫大卫说，现实一直都是这种感觉吗？

00:15:54.800 --> 00:16:06.260
<v Sean Cole>You know when they say the answer is located within the question? This was one of those times.
<v Sean Cole>你知道人们常说答案就在问题中吗？这就是那种时刻之一。

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:34.100
<v Sean Cole>Sarah called her sister, who's a doctor, and who said, yeah, it's a concussion. All the classic symptoms-- oversensitivity to noise and light, confusion, wooziness. She told Sarah to just take it easy. Probably, she'd be back to normal in no time. But that's not what happened. Sarah had all of those symptoms, plus fatigue, occasional nausea, and terrible migraines on and off for the next 3 and 1/2 years.
<v Sean Cole>莎拉给做医生的姐姐打了电话，对方说，是的，是脑震荡。所有典型的症状——对噪音和光线过敏、困惑、眩晕。她告诉莎拉只需要静养。大概很快就能恢复正常。但事实并非如此。莎拉有所有这些症状，加上疲劳、偶尔恶心，以及在接下来的3年半里断断续续的剧烈偏头痛。

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:55.240
<v Sean Cole>Some days, she'd just lay in a dark room for hours because the headaches were too much. The light sensitivity also meant limiting her screen time, so she could barely work. She was actually in the middle of adapting the novel Little Women into a screenplay when she got hit on the head, that gig that ultimately went to Greta Gerwig instead. Also very challenging-- just hanging out with friends and family.
<v Sean Cole>有些日子，她只是在黑暗的房间里躺上几个小时，因为头痛得太厉害。对光敏感也意味着限制使用屏幕的时间，所以她几乎无法工作。当她头部被砸时，她实际上正在改编小说《小妇人》的剧本，那份工作最终落到了格蕾塔·葛韦格手里。这非常具有挑战性——即使只是和朋友家人待在一起也是如此。

00:16:55.240 --> 00:17:12.910
<v Sarah Polley>Like in a conversation at dinner, where there's things going on, I haven't been able to follow what people are saying. And I was really good at faking it, but half the time, I didn't know what the hell was going on. And it was scary and embarrassing.
<v Sarah Polley>就像晚餐时的谈话，周围有很多事发生时，我无法跟上人们在说什么。我非常擅长伪装，但有一半的时间，我根本不知道到底发生了什么。这很可怕，也很尴尬。

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:31.330
<v Sean Cole>But the biggest heartbreak was not being able to care for her kids that much, especially in the first year. Couldn't take them to school, couldn't go to their events. She just became less and less involved with their lives. And this is the story of how she made her way out of that hole, taking a path that was totally unexpected.
<v Sean Cole>但最大的心碎是无法过多照顾自己的孩子，尤其是在第一年。无法送他们上学，无法参加他们的活动。她参与他们生活的程度越来越低。这就是她如何走出那个深渊的故事，走上了一条完全出乎意料的道路。

00:17:31.330 --> 00:17:42.370
<v Sean Cole>At first, she was just marching herself around to doctor after doctor. She actually published an essay in a book of essays about all of this. This part's a good summation of the different treatments she tried, so I asked her to read it.
<v Sean Cole>起初，她只是把自己带去见一位又一位医生。她实际上在一部论文集中发表了一篇关于所有这些经历的文章。这一部分总结了她尝试过的不同治疗方法，所以我请她读一读。

00:17:42.370 --> 00:18:03.710
<v Sarah Polley>"I go to a neurofeedback specialist who tells me, after I spent an exhausting day watching balls bounce around on a computer screen, that I am in the one percentile for attention span for my age and gender. This will haunt me for years. A nutritionist who tells me to stop eating gluten and dairy and a chiropractor who apparently helped cure hockey player Sidney Crosby."
<v Sarah Polley>“我去了一位神经反馈专家那里，他在我花费了令人精疲力竭的一天观看球体在电脑屏幕上弹跳后告诉我，我的注意力集中时间在我的年龄和性别中处于倒数第一位。这将困扰我多年。还有一位营养师告诉我停止吃麸质和乳制品，以及一位据说治愈了冰球运动员西德尼·克罗斯比的脊椎按摩师。”

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:10.460
<v Sean Cole>Sidney Crosby is a hockey legend, just to say. In 2011, he was diagnosed with a concussion that famously benched him for about a year.
<v Sean Cole>西德尼·克罗斯比是冰球传奇人物，顺便提一下。2011年，他被诊断出患有脑震荡，这让他被迫休战了大约一年，这件事很有名。

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:32.550
<v Sarah Polley>"I will later realize that almost everyone out there specializing in concussions has a rumor swirling around them that they cured Sidney Crosby. After a while, whenever anyone starts to rave about a concussion miracle worker they know, I say, let me guess-- he treated Sidney Crosby. The answer is invariably yes."
<v Sarah Polley>“后来我意识到，几乎所有专注于脑震荡的人身上都流传着一种谣言，说他们治愈了西德尼·克罗斯比。一段时间后，每当有人开始热烈地谈论他们认识的脑震荡神医时，我会说，让我猜猜——他治疗过西德尼·克罗斯比。答案总是肯定的。”

00:18:32.550 --> 00:18:53.700
<v Sean Cole>Out of the tangle of advice, much of it conflicting, stuck this one common thread, that Sarah should do as much as she felt like she could handle, but when she reached the point where she couldn't tolerate the stimulation or the sunlight or traffic sounds or whatever it was, then back off. Don't try to push yourself past the point where your body is telling you to quit, and make sure to get lots of rest.
<v Sean Cole>在混乱的建议中，很多都是相互矛盾的，但有一条共同点：莎拉应该尽可能多地做她觉得能应付的事情，但当她达到无法忍受刺激、阳光或交通噪音等任何东西的临界点时，就退回来。不要试图强迫自己超过身体告诉你放弃的极限，并确保获得充分的休息。

00:18:53.700 --> 00:19:13.230
<v Sean Cole>It's not exactly rare, this condition. It's been referred to as post-concussion syndrome. Maybe 15% or 20% of people with concussions develop some form of it. In fact, Sarah's friend Meredith happened to be dealing with it at the same time. They'd compare notes. Meredith always wore wraparound shades and wide-brimmed hats when she went outside.
<v Sean Cole>这种情况并不罕见。它被称为脑震荡后综合征。也许15%或20%的脑震荡患者会产生某种形式的症状。事实上，莎拉的朋友梅雷迪斯正好在同一时间处理这种情况。她们会比较记录。梅雷迪斯出门时总是戴着遮阳眼镜和宽檐帽。

00:19:13.230 --> 00:19:45.970
<v Sean Cole>And somewhere around year 3, Sarah got almost used to her limitations. She kept detailed notes about her symptoms-- when they showed up, how severe they were. And in what she calls an irrational fit of optimism, she and David decide to have a third kid, which would have been a huge load for anyone. But for Sarah, it was insurmountable. She felt just as bad as she did on day one and starts to give up on ever being recognizably herself again.
<v Sean Cole>在大约第3年，莎拉几乎习惯了她的局限。她记录了关于自己症状的详细记录——它们何时出现，严重程度如何。怀着一种她自己称之为非理性的乐观，她和大卫决定生第三个孩子，这对任何人来说都是巨大的负担。但对莎拉来说，这是无法逾越的。她感觉和第一天一样糟糕，并开始放弃恢复原本自己的希望。

00:19:45.970 --> 00:20:04.990
<v Sean Cole>And then, one day, 3 and 1/2 years in, Sarah sees Meredith, her concussion buddy, on the street, except now, out of nowhere, Meredith's fine. No wraparound shades, no wide-brimmed hat. She basically says she was cured by this doctor in Pittsburgh, Dr. Michael Collins.
<v Sean Cole>然后有一天，在第3年半，莎拉在街上看到了她的脑震荡伙伴梅雷迪斯，除了现在，不知从哪里冒出来的，梅雷迪斯好了。没有遮阳眼镜，没有宽檐帽。她基本上说她是匹兹堡的一位医生，迈克尔·柯林斯医生治愈的。

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:24.790
<v Sean Cole>And the more Sarah learned about him, the more snake oily the whole thing sounded. There's just so much debate around the science of concussions, and the cure seems so elusive. And now, here is this guy who was completely confident that he could fix what was wrong, that there was a method to it. Plus, the way his patients felt about him seemed cultish and weird.
<v Sean Cole>莎拉对他了解得越多，整件事听起来就越像江湖骗子。关于脑震荡的科学争论太多了，治疗方法似乎难以捉摸。而现在，这个家伙却完全自信他能解决她的问题，说有一套方法。此外，他的病人们对他那种崇拜式的态度显得很奇怪。

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:41.350
<v Sean Cole>And besides being skeptical, Sarah, who, remember, is Canadian and explicitly asked me to include this, she didn't want to buy into the notion that health care in the US is superior to what you could get in Canada-- and literally buy in. She's like, nobody should have to pay for health care. It made her feel gross.
<v Sean Cole>除了持怀疑态度，莎拉——记得她是加拿大人，特意要求我包括这一点——不想认同美国的医疗保健优于加拿大这一观点——并且确实在购买它。她觉得，没有人应该为医疗保健买单。这让她感到恶心。

00:20:41.350 --> 00:21:02.750
<v Sean Cole>But all of that said, she had tried everything else. Maybe a little snake oil was what she needed. So she booked an appointment and brought her friend, Kate, down with her to Pittsburgh to sit with her and take notes on what the doctor said so she'd remember. The trip itself was hugely taxing for her. And when the time came to see Dr. Collins, he was not what she expected.
<v Sean Cole>但话虽如此，她尝试了所有其他办法。也许一点江湖骗术正是她所需要的。于是她预约了医生，带上她的朋友凯特一起去了匹兹堡，坐在旁边记录医生说的话，以便她能记住。这趟旅行本身对她来说就极其吃力。当见到柯林斯医生时，他并不是她预想的那样。

00:21:02.750 --> 00:21:29.450
<v Sarah Polley>He kind of burst into the room in whatever a Canadian stereotypical image of an American is. It was like America came into the room. He was really direct, didn't hold back. He was loud. He didn't have a lot of time for niceties. I literally felt like Kate and I were like, our hair is being blown back by the sheer energy of this man.
<v Sarah Polley>他简直是冲进了房间，带着任何加拿大人对美国人的典型印象。就像美国直接走进了房间。他非常直接，毫不保留。他嗓门大。他没有太多时间去客套。我真的觉得凯特和我，我们的头发都被这个男人的纯粹能量吹到了后面。

00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:33.670
<v Sean Cole>He immediately points at Kate, who's quietly taking notes in the corner.
<v Sean Cole>他立刻指着躲在角落里安静记笔记的凯特。

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:44.240
<v Sarah Polley>That's not going to help. I read all your husband's notes. He's taking all these notes about your symptoms. That's not going to help. And he yelled at Kate, and he was like, put that pen down. You're not helping. And Kate dropped her pen and was like--
<v Sarah Polley>那没用。我读了所有你丈夫的笔记。他在记所有关于你症状的笔记。那没用。他冲凯特喊道，就像，把那支笔放下。你帮不上忙。凯特放下笔，就像——

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:47.000
<v Sean Cole>And he's talking at that volume of voice? Like--
<v Sean Cole>他是用那个音量说话的吗？比如——

00:21:47.000 --> 00:22:02.010
<v Sarah Polley>Yeah, yeah, yeah. No, he's a really intense guy. And I mean, I was used to a lot of "listen to your body," "be gentle with yourself." And he was like, stop tracking your symptoms. You're not helping yourself by tracking your symptoms, which I was extremely good at.
<v Sarah Polley>是的，是的，是的。不，他是个非常强硬的人。我的意思是，我习惯了很多“倾听你的身体”、“对自己温柔一点”之类的建议。而他却说，停止追踪你的症状。通过追踪你的症状，你对自己一点帮助都没有，而我在这方面非常擅长。

00:22:02.010 --> 00:22:17.450
<v Sarah Polley>And I mean, he's basically like-- I don't know. I don't play sports. It's what I imagine an American football coach is like. The first thing he had said was, we're going to have to pin our ears and go after this thing, which is like my kid's favorite expression. We're going to have to pin our ears and go after this thing.
<v Sarah Polley>我的意思是，他基本上就像——我不知道。我不参加体育运动。这是我想象中美式橄榄球教练的样子。他说的第一件事是，我们得咬紧牙关，去对抗这个东西，这正是我孩子最喜欢的表达。我们得咬紧牙关，去对抗这个东西。

00:22:17.450 --> 00:22:25.170
<v Sean Cole>He basically told Sarah that she was thinking about her concussion as something she needed to make room for in her life, which was exactly wrong.
<v Sean Cole>他基本上告诉莎拉，她把脑震荡想成了她生活中需要腾出空间来容纳的东西，这完全是错的。

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:39.960
<v Sarah Polley>He said, if there's one thing I need you to remember from this meeting, if you don't remember anything else-- and there was a possibility I wouldn't, because Kate wasn't allowed to take notes because he yelled at her. If there's only one thing you remember from this meeting today, it's this-- run towards the danger.
<v Sarah Polley>他说，如果有什么事是我需要你从这次会面中记住的，如果你记不住别的什么——而且很有可能我记不住，因为凯特不被允许做笔记，因为他冲她喊了。如果你今天从这次会面中只能记住一件事，那就是这个——冲向危险。

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:41.540
<v Sean Cole>Run towards the danger.
<v Sean Cole>冲向危险。

00:22:41.540 --> 00:23:01.020
<v Sarah Polley>Anything that's traditionally triggered your symptoms, you're doing more of. So now, if you're watching TV with someone, you have to ask if it's loud enough for them, because they might be keeping it lower for your concussion. You have to ask if the room is bright enough for other people because everyone's probably gotten to a habit of lower light for you and your concussion.
<v Sarah Polley>任何传统上触发你症状的事情，你都要做更多。所以现在，如果你和别人一起看电视，你必须问他们音量够不够大，因为他们可能为了你的脑震荡把音量调低了。你必须问房间对其他人来说够不够亮，因为大家可能已经习惯了为你和你的脑震荡调低灯光。

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:13.650
<v Sarah Polley>You have to basically just take off all the band-aids. And what I was not allowed to do was-- he was like, if I hear that you're having a nap, ever, I will yell at you. If you have a nap in the day, I will yell at you.
<v Sarah Polley>你必须基本上摘掉所有的绷带。我被禁止做的是——他说，如果我听说你睡午觉，哪怕一次，我就冲你喊。如果你白天睡午觉，我就冲你喊。

00:23:13.650 --> 00:23:15.140
<v Sarah Polley>[LAUGHTER]
<v Sarah Polley>（笑声）

00:23:15.140 --> 00:23:40.010
<v Sarah Polley>And he meant it. And I was like, holy shit. What if I'm trying to make breakfast for my kids, breastfeed my baby, I'm alone at home, I'm trying to get them all off to school? Just thinking about that gives me a migraine. I mean, what if I get a migraine if I'm overextending myself in that way, and then I can't function for the rest of the day? And he just looked at me and yelled, attack, attack, attack!
<v Sarah Polley>他是认真的。我当时想，天哪。如果我正在尝试为孩子们做早餐，给婴儿哺乳，我一个人在家，试图让他们都去上学呢？光是想想这些就让我偏头痛。我的意思是，如果我那样过度劳累导致偏头痛，然后余下的一天都无法正常工作怎么办？他只是看着我，喊道，攻击，攻击，攻击！

00:23:40.010 --> 00:23:41.420
<v Sean Cole>[LAUGHS]
<v Sean Cole>（笑）

00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:48.510
<v Sarah Polley>It was so arresting.
<v Sarah Polley>这太引人注目了。

00:23:48.510 --> 00:24:13.140
<v Sean Cole>Remember, this was, like, the opposite of what every other doctor had told her. Everybody else was like, don't push past the point of your own tolerance. And Dr. Collins was like, push past the point of your own tolerance. Keep going. Sarah asked him how long it would take for her to get better, and he said, hmm, a pretty straightforward case, maybe four to six weeks, which is when she became firmly convinced that he was a charlatan.
<v Sean Cole>记住，这就像是其他每一位医生告诉她的完全相反的事情。其他每个人都说，不要超过你耐受力的极限。而柯林斯医生却说，超过你的耐受极限。继续前进。莎拉问他她需要多久才能好转，他说，嗯，一个相当直截了当的病例，大概四到六周，就在那时，她坚信他是个骗子。

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:34.110
<v Sean Cole>But he's not a charlatan. For all of the ear pinning and attack, attack, attack stuff, Dr. Michael Collins is a world-renowned leading expert in concussions. He's a clinical neuropsychologist, not an MD. But he helped develop a special diagnostic tool to test the severity and type of concussion. Yes, there are different types of concussions-- six of them, in fact.
<v Sean Cole>但他不是骗子。尽管有咬牙、攻击、攻击、攻击那些东西，迈克尔·柯林斯医生是世界公认的脑震荡领先专家。他是临床神经心理学家，不是医学博士。但他参与开发了一种专门的诊断工具来测试脑震荡的严重程度和类型。是的，脑震荡确实有不同的类型——事实上，有六种。

00:24:34.110 --> 00:24:45.160
<v Sean Cole>And he's worked on hundreds of research articles and important studies in the field. And the way his team approaches treatment is becoming more and more accepted, both here and in other countries. Oh, and also--
<v Sean Cole>他参与了数百篇研究文章和领域内的重要研究。他的团队处理治疗的方法正变得越来越被认可，无论是在这里还是在其他国家。哦，对了，还有——

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:52.270
<v Michael Collins>We're making progress in Sid's case, and I anticipate Sid returning to hockey and playing well in the future.
<v Michael Collins>我们在西德的情况上取得了进展，我预计西德会回到冰球场，在未来打得很好。

00:24:52.270 --> 00:24:59.480
<v Sean Cole>--he actually did treat the hockey player Sidney Crosby. This is from a press conference where Sidney is literally sitting two chairs away from him.
<v Sean Cole>——他确实治疗过冰球运动员西德尼·克罗斯比。这是一场新闻发布会，西德就坐在离他两张椅子的位置。

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:10.030
<v Michael Collins>Yesterday, actually, I assessed Sid. And I can tell you that his data is the best we've seen. It is approaching--
<v Michael Collins>昨天，我实际上评估了西德。我可以告诉你，他的数据是我们见过的最好的。它正在接近——

00:25:10.030 --> 00:25:16.760
<v Sean Cole>I got in touch with Michael Collins, or Mickey to people who know him. First and foremost, I asked him what was going on with all the yelling.
<v Sean Cole>我和迈克尔·柯林斯取得了联系，或者说熟悉他的人称他为米奇。首先，我问他那一阵阵的喊叫是怎么回事。

00:25:16.760 --> 00:25:19.120
<v Michael Collins>I was having a tough day that day.
<v Michael Collins>那天我过得很艰难。

00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:33.460
<v Sean Cole>He said he'd been running hours behind schedule on some really complicated cases before seeing Sarah. But beyond that, he says he felt like he needed to get through to her, literally loud and clear, that noise and stimulation were not the enemy.
<v Sean Cole>他说他在见到莎拉之前，一些非常复杂的病例让他进度落后了几个小时。但除此之外，他说他觉得他需要让她明白，声音和刺激不是敌人，一定要响亮而清晰。

00:25:33.460 --> 00:25:47.590
<v Sean Cole>Put very simply, the bash on Sarah's head injured this part of her brain that allows you to interpret motion and busy environments. So the system gets overloaded and can't manage everything that's flying at it. And the injury also caused anxiety.
<v Sean Cole>简单来说，莎拉头部受到的重创伤害了她大脑中允许你解读运动和忙碌环境的那一部分。所以系统过载了，无法处理飞向它的一切。这次伤害也导致了焦虑。

00:25:47.590 --> 00:26:14.790
<v Sean Cole>But here's the weird part-- whenever Sarah felt anxiety about, like, going to a social event, that anxiety alone could also trigger her physical concussion symptoms-- the brain fog, wooziness, and confusion. It's a two-way conversation. And so you run toward the danger to interrupt that conversation. You just ignore your anxiety about the symptoms, and so the anxiety and the symptoms both start to fade.
<v Sean Cole>但奇怪的地方在于——每当莎拉感到焦虑时，比如要去参加一个社交活动，仅仅那种焦虑本身就能引发她的身体脑震荡症状——脑雾、头晕和混乱。这是一个双向的对话。所以你冲向危险是为了打断那种对话。你只是忽略了对症状的焦虑，焦虑和症状就开始同时消退了。

00:26:14.790 --> 00:26:46.860
<v Michael Collins>She had the type of concussion where it's not rest that gets someone better. It's about rehabituation and retraining. And so to make sure she was aware of that-- because every instinct in your body when you're going through this is to back off things. And so yeah, a part of it was I was having a busy day, but a part of it was she needed to hear it. And she needed to understand that there's only one way out, and that's through it. We actually wanted her to have symptoms. We wanted her to feel it.
<v Michael Collins>她患有的那种脑震荡并不是靠休息就能好的。而是关于重新习惯化和重新训练。所以为了确保她意识到这一点——因为当你经历这些时，身体里的每一个本能都是去逃避事情。所以是的，部分原因是我那天很忙，但部分原因是她需要听到这些。她需要明白只有一条出路，那就是直面它。我们实际上想让她有症状。我们想让她感觉到它。

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:59.570
<v Sean Cole>And just to say, he's not yell-y with every patient. And some patients have completely different kinds of concussions. Please consult a physician before treating your own concussion by going to a Five Finger Death Punch concert.
<v Sean Cole>顺便提一下，他对每个病人都不这样。有些病人有完全不同类型的脑震荡。在通过去参加Five Finger Death Punch演唱会治疗你自己的脑震荡之前，请务必咨询医生。

00:26:59.570 --> 00:27:21.520
<v Sean Cole>But with Sarah, he told her, we're going to put you on an intensive retraining program-- exercise, including very specific exercises to get that motion interpretation system-- it's called the vestibular system-- working again, and all the classic exposure therapy you just heard about. Dr. Collins warned her that this would be very uncomfortable at first. He was not lying.
<v Sean Cole>但对于莎拉，他告诉她，我们要让你进行强化重新训练计划——运动，包括非常具体的练习，让那种运动解读系统——即所谓的前庭系统——重新工作，以及你刚才听到的所有经典的暴露疗法。柯林斯医生警告她，起初会非常不舒服。他没撒谎。

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:32.460
<v Sarah Polley>I was in agony. Everything hurt. I had worse brain fog at times. It was exhausting. It made me nauseous. It gave me headaches.
<v Sarah Polley>我痛苦不堪。一切都疼。有时脑雾更严重。这令人精疲力竭。它让我恶心。它让我头痛。

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:40.470
<v Sean Cole>Take, for example, a simple visit to the grocery store. For years, her husband had been doing most of the shopping, but now, shopping was prescribed.
<v Sean Cole>举个例子，去杂货店买东西。多年来，她丈夫承担了大部分购物，但现在，购物成了处方。

00:27:40.470 --> 00:28:07.880
<v Sarah Polley>Just the number of things that were happening, like the bright lights, the music, needing to make decisions about what to pull off the shelf, looking at a list, which, again, when you can see how much work each of those steps takes for your brain to process and execute, it is an explosion of exhaustion and pain. And certainly, I remember being at a kids' concert, like a kids' rock concert in the gym.
<v Sarah Polley>光是发生的事情的数量，比如明亮的灯光、音乐，需要决定从货架上拿什么，看清单，再次强调，当你能看到大脑处理和执行每一个步骤需要付出多少工作时，那简直是精疲力竭和痛苦的爆发。当然，我记得在一个儿童音乐会上，比如在体育馆里举办的儿童摇滚音乐会。

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:12.990
<v Sean Cole>A thing that I think that even people without hypersensitivity to loud noises do not necessarily enjoy.
<v Sean Cole>我想即使是对噪音没有过度敏感的人也不一定喜欢这种活动。

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:30.050
<v Sarah Polley>And I just wanted to die. And it was like, it must have been such a sight because you position your face in a certain kind of way at a kids' performance, which I was incapable of doing.
<v Sarah Polley>我当时简直想死。那场面一定很滑稽，因为你在儿童表演时会以某种方式控制自己的面部表情，而我完全做不到。

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:40.860
<v Sarah Polley>Yeah, my brain was exploding a lot in those first few weeks. And then there was this moment where we'd been invited out.
<v Sarah Polley>是的，在最初的几周里，我的大脑经常爆炸。然后有那么一个时刻，我们被邀请出去了。

00:28:40.860 --> 00:28:43.110
<v Sean Cole>This is maybe two or three weeks into the treatment.
<v Sean Cole>这大概是在治疗两三周后。

00:28:43.110 --> 00:29:08.370
<v Sarah Polley>David had this new colleague and his partner, and we went to a restaurant, and I was bringing my breastfeeding baby into a restaurant while having to socialize with new people, with noise and light. And I said yes because I had to, because it was part of my program to say yes to these things. And I realized halfway through the conversation that I had been following the whole conversation--
<v Sarah Polley>大卫有了个新同事和他的伴侣，我们去了一家餐馆，我必须一边给哺乳期的婴儿喂奶，一边与新朋友社交，还要应对噪音和光线。我答应了，因为我必须答应，因为向这些事情说“是”是我的计划的一部分。谈话进行到一半，我意识到我一直在跟上整个谈话——

00:29:08.370 --> 00:29:08.810
<v Sean Cole>Hmm!
<v Sean Cole>嗯！

00:29:08.810 --> 00:29:31.520
<v Sarah Polley>--and that I wasn't lost, and my head didn't hurt, and I wasn't confused. And I expressed that to my husband on the walk home. And as I did, I started sobbing uncontrollably, because I suddenly realized, this is what it used to feel like to be in my brain.
<v Sarah Polley>——我没有迷失，头不疼，也没有困惑。我在回家的路上向我丈夫表达了这一点。就在那一刻，我开始无法抑制地啜泣，因为我突然意识到，这就是曾经作为正常大脑的感觉。

00:29:31.520 --> 00:29:53.560
<v Sarah Polley>And I didn't even know how far off I was of being myself or being fully functioning. And so it was both an incredible moment of knowing, oh, I am going to get better, actually, and also real grief for the last 3 and 1/2 years, and also just my lack of even having known what I'd lost.
<v Sarah Polley>我甚至不知道自己离做回自己或完全正常运作有多远。所以那一刻既是一个意识到“噢，我其实会好起来”的绝妙瞬间，也是对自己过去3年半的真正哀悼，也是对自己甚至不知道自己失去了什么的感伤。

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:58.600
<v Sean Cole>And by the end of six weeks, just like the doctor had said, she was totally cured.
<v Sean Cole>到六周结束时，就像医生说的那样，她彻底治愈了。

00:29:58.600 --> 00:30:16.970
<v Sarah Polley>It honestly just felt like a veil lifting off the world, like I'd been behind this screen that I didn't know I was behind. And my vision had adjusted to everything being a bit blurry. And then suddenly, everything was clear and in technicolor and beautiful.
<v Sarah Polley>真的感觉就像一层面纱从世界上揭开，就像我一直待在自己不知道身处的屏幕后面。而我的视力已经适应了一切变得有点模糊。然后突然间，一切都变得清晰、色彩鲜艳且美丽。

00:30:16.970 --> 00:30:39.980
<v Sean Cole>It's been five years since Sarah recovered. And not only is she symptom-free, she actually gained something that I never would have expected, and is the entire reason I wanted you to hear this story. That whole run towards the danger thing, it's pervaded the rest of her life. Instead of being all squirrely and avoidant when a big challenge comes along, now she's like, OK, bring it on.
<v Sean Cole>莎拉康复已经五年了。不仅症状全无，她实际上还获得了一些我从未预料到的东西，这就是我希望你们听这个故事的全部原因。那种“冲向危险”的事情，已经渗透到了她生活的其他方面。当遇到巨大的挑战时，她不再胆怯和逃避，现在她心想，好吧，放马过来吧。

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:59.480
<v Sarah Polley>I do all kinds of things now that I am terrified of and that I would never do. Like, I now, if I'm asked to be on stage, it's very likely I will say yes because it's so hard for me. And I know the more I do it and the more I work with it, the better it's going to get. So I don't really avoid anything that bugs me anymore.
<v Sarah Polley>我现在做各种让我感到恐惧、以前绝不会做的事情。比如，现在如果有人请我上台表演，很可能我会答应，因为这对我很困难。我知道我做得越多，处理得越多，它就会变得越好。所以，我不再真正逃避任何让我烦恼的事情了。

00:30:59.480 --> 00:31:02.880
<v Sarah Polley>I am so very honored to be asked to speak here today--
<v Sarah Polley>能受邀今天在这里演讲，我深感荣幸——

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:12.770
<v Sean Cole>This is Sarah addressing graduates at the University of Toronto just a couple of months ago. She told the crowd she was terrified of being on stage, even as she was onstage speaking to them.
<v Sean Cole>这是莎拉几个月前在多伦多大学向毕业生演讲的情景。她告诉人群，即使在台上向他们演讲，她也感到非常恐惧。

00:31:12.770 --> 00:31:18.280
<v Sarah Polley>There are many things to be legitimately afraid of. Don't worry, I won't list them.
<v Sarah Polley>有很多事情是真正值得恐惧的。别担心，我不会列举它们。

00:31:18.280 --> 00:31:39.720
<v Sean Cole>She's also taken on huge projects that would have felt insurmountable to her before the concussion happened, like the movie Women Talking, which won her an Oscar last year for her screenplay. And that book I mentioned before, it's called Run Towards the Danger. And it's full of deeply personal, sometimes really upsetting, stories from her life that she'd always been scared to tell.
<v Sean Cole>她还承担了巨大的项目，这些项目在脑震荡发生前对她来说是无法逾越的，比如电影《女性的谈话》，该片去年为她的剧本赢得了奥斯卡奖。我之前提到那本书，书名叫《冲向危险》。里面充满了她生活中深度个人、有时非常令人不安的故事，这些故事她一直害怕讲出来。

00:31:39.720 --> 00:31:51.040
<v Sean Cole>And is it literally, if you were going into some sort of uncomfortable situation, that you're thinking of the words "run towards the danger" now?
<v Sean Cole>那是字面意思吗，如果你要进入某种不舒服的境地，你现在脑子里会想到“冲向危险”这几个字吗？

00:31:51.040 --> 00:32:11.280
